.TH PULSE 1 "RazionOS 0.1 Alpha"
.SH NAME
pulse \- safe natural-language operating-system command interface
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pulse
[\fB\-\-plan\fP]
[\fB\-\-confirm\fP]
[\fB\-\-json\fP]
[\fIREQUEST\fP]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Razion Pulse
maps a natural-language request to a bounded operating-system action.
It is a command interface, not a chatbot.
Actions are implemented through fixed native APIs; request text and AI
provider output are never executed by a shell.
.PP
When no request is supplied, Pulse reads one request interactively.
Read-only actions may run immediately.
Actions that modify user data require explicit confirmation.
Privileged and unavailable actions remain proposals and are not executed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-plan
Display the validated proposal without executing it.
.TP
.B \-\-confirm
Explicitly approve an available change action after reviewing its target.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Display a machine-readable proposal and imply
.BR \-\-plan .
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B pulse "Open Downloads"
Open the user's Downloads directory with the native file browser.
.TP
.B pulse "Check memory usage"
Read and summarize the kernel memory information.
.TP
.B pulse \-\-plan "Create a Python project"
Preview the fixed project target and required confirmation.
.TP
.B pulse \-\-confirm "Create a Python project"
Create the project only if the target does not already exist.
.SH SECURITY
Pulse validates all actions against a fixed action vocabulary.
It never evaluates request text as a command.
Provider-assisted classification is local-only by default and its output must
match a known action identifier.
Privileged actions are denied until a dedicated native broker exists.
Audit records contain metadata only and are stored in
.IR ~/.razion/pulse-audit.log .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR razion-ai-status (1)
